  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure over the Gulf of Maine departs as high \npressure builds over the area.\n\nThe former nor'easter, now weak low pressure, over the Gulf of Maine \ncontinues to depart late tonight. High pressure centered near West \nVirginia will build east late tonight and into Wednesday. \n\nClouds over the area will gradually scatter out late tonight. With \nabundant low level moisture over the area and winds becoming nearly \ncalm, patchy fog will develop during the early morning hours. Fog \nwill lift and dissipate Wednesday morning, giving way to mostly \nsunny skies. Mild with slightly above normal temperatures on \nWednesday with highs in the mid to upper 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tidal flooding continues to recede in the back bays \nof New Jersey and Delaware.\n\nTidal levels continue to decline and recede as offshore flow \ncontinues to allow the back bays to drain. While some of the \nback bays of Barnegat Bay, Rehoboth Bay, Jefferson Creek and \nLittle Assawoman Bay may see some spotty minor tidal flooding\ncontinue through Wednesday, the bulk of the threat has now\nended. As a result, the Coastal Flood Advisories for Ocean \nCounty, NJ and Sussex County, DE will allow to expire at 9 PM\ntonight.\n\nOutside of these areas, a few locales may touch spotty minor \nwith tonight's high tide cycle, otherwise, no further tidal \nflooding is expected elsewhere.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled weather possible Friday through\nSunday.\n\nA cold front is forecast to move across the area late Friday into \nFriday night. As it moves into the area, there will be a chance of \nshowers. However, as the vorticity impulse aloft weakens, the \nshowers may dissipate for many areas as the move eastward. However, \nadditional rain fall is expected to move back into the area Saturday \nnight into Sunday. An area of low pressure is forecast to develop \nalong the front as it stalls to our south Saturday, then move \nnorthward across the area through Sunday. Showers will increase \nSaturday night into Sunday as a couple of short wave/vorticity \nimpulses move into the area, along with the surface low\npressure.",
